{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>I will serve you seven years for Rachel.</b> There is no doubt that the righteous man would not marry a woman to establish children if he did not have the means to provide for her — especially food and clothing — as the Sages said (Yevamot 63a): \"A man may marry several women, provided he has the means to maintain them.\" And likewise one should not suppose that Laban, who was a man of wealth, would give him his daughters if he did not have the means to provide for them. If so, when he said כִּי בְמַקְלִי עָבַרְתִּי (Genesis 32:11), the intent was that at that time he did not have livestock, nor any way to engage in trade to profit and sustain himself thereby. And the matter of the labor for Rachel's sake was to pay a bride-price, as was their custom — that they would give a bride-price to the father so that he would give his daughter, as stated: אִם מָאֵן יְמָאֵן אָבִיהָ לְתִתָּהּ לוֹ כֶּסֶף יִשְׁקֹל כְּמֹהַר הַבְּתוּלוֹת (Exodus 22:16). And what they said afterward, כִּי מְכָרָנוּ (Genesis 31:15), was because they thought, in humility, that the bride-price was more than was fitting for them." "<b>Your younger daughter.</b> For within the seven years there will now be a fitting time for marriage, and you can meanwhile give the elder one in marriage."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:32:23.152428012Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}